WEBVTT

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:16.380
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. And I am joined in the studio by M. Gessen.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是《此生美国》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。和我一起在录音室里的是玛莎·盖森。

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:16.960
<v Ira Glass>Hello.
<v Ira Glass>接下来事情发生了。那件事，一颗在我家族中间爆炸的炸弹。

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:17.700
<v M. Gessen>Hi, Ira.
<v M. Gessen>嗨，艾拉。

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.220
<v Ira Glass>It's so nice to have you back here.
<v Ira Glass>很高兴你能回到这里。

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:20.760
<v M. Gessen>It's always lovely to be here.
<v M. Gessen>能来到这里总是很开心。

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:27.160
<v Ira Glass>And the story that you're about to tell today is one that you've been telling for years?
<v Ira Glass>你今天要讲的故事，是已经讲了好几年的故事吗？

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:38.920
<v M. Gessen>Yeah. First it was just, there was something weird going on in my family, but also insane ways that my family talks about these crazy events.
<v M. Gessen>对。起初只是觉得我家里发生了一些怪事，而且我家人谈论这些疯狂事件的方式也极其荒唐。

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:50.480
<v Ira Glass>And is this story a story that when you would tell it to friends and loved ones, was it a funny story?
<v Ira Glass>当你把这个故事讲给朋友和亲人听时，这是一个好笑的故事吗？

00:00:50.480 --> 00:01:09.840
<v M. Gessen>I hesitate to say that it was a funny story, but yes. Yes, Ira, it was a funny story. And I mean, maybe that's also just the only way that we can deal with things that are unbelievable. It wasn't until I started reporting it that I realized how horrible the story actually was.
<v M. Gessen>我很难说这是一个好笑的故事，但确实是。是的，艾拉，这是一个好笑的故事。我的意思是，也许这也是我们应对那些难以置信的事情的唯一方式。直到我开始对此进行深度报道，我才意识到这个故事实际上有多么可怕。

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:25.740
<v Ira Glass>And when you started to report it-- this was years ago-- originally this was going to be a story for This American Life. And then at some point it just got too big. Like, it was like, we cannot contain this in one episode of our show. And you turned it into this podcast with Serial.
<v Ira Glass>当你开始报道它时——这已经是多年前的事了——最初这本该是《此生美国》的一个故事。但后来在某个时候，它变得太庞大了。就像是，我们无法把它塞进节目的单集里。于是你把它做成了《Serial》的播客。

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:26.440
<v M. Gessen>Yes.
<v M. Gessen>是的。

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:54.240
<v Ira Glass>And it's now a five-part series with Serial that was released this week. And you've been doing read throughs of drafts as you've been writing drafts that I've sat in on. And I just want to say, I just, I love this show and feel like this show is so different from other podcasts that I have heard in a bunch of interesting ways. And what we're going to do today on our program is we're going to walk through enough of the story so that listeners here can hear what I'm hearing in it. And then if they want, they can go and listen to the whole thing.
<v Ira Glass>现在这是《Serial》推出的一部五集系列节目，在本周刚刚发布。在你写稿子的过程中，我也一直在读你写好的初稿。我想说，我真的很喜欢这个节目，我觉得它在很多有趣方面都与我听过的其他播客大不相同。我们今天在节目里要做的是，梳理出足够多的故事内容，让这里的听众能听到我在其中听到的一切。然后如果他们愿意，可以去听完整的节目。

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:08.580
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, I'm Ira Glass. That's going to be our show today, and we're going to begin by playing the first episode of the series, which is almost like a prologue and sets the whole thing up. Is there anything else that we should say before we play that?
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是《此生美国》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。这就是我们今天的节目，我们将从播放该系列的第一集开始，它几乎就像是一个序幕，为整个故事奠定了基调。在我们播放之前，还有什么需要补充的吗？

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.800
<v M. Gessen>No, I think we can jump in.
<v M. Gessen>没有，我觉得我们可以直接开始了。

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.120
<v Ira Glass>OK, let's just jump right in with that.
<v Ira Glass>好的，那我们就直接切入正题吧。

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:42.600
<v M. Gessen>My family, if I had to give it an adjective, is elastic. 45 years ago, my parents, my little brother, and I came over to this country from the Soviet Union, extending the family across continents. Over the decades, the family, my father really, stretched to absorb spouses, in-laws, even though they spoke a different language, children, both biological and adopted. Ex-spouses who chose to stick around, and eventually grandchildren.
<v M. Gessen>我的家庭，如果要用一个形容词来概括，那就是极具弹性。45年前，我的父母、我的小弟弟和我从苏联来到这个国家，将家族的枝叶延伸到了跨越大陆的地方。几十年间，这个家庭——准确地说是我的父亲——不断扩展以接纳新成员：配偶、姻亲（尽管他们说着不同的语言）、亲生及收养的孩子、选择留下来并最终成为家属的前配偶，以及后来的孙辈们。

00:02:42.600 --> 00:03:01.440
<v M. Gessen>Over those same decades, as in any family, people made bad decisions, said things they hoped no one would remember, got mad at each other, held grudges, came around, and the family stretched as needed. And then it snapped. Someone did something that bad, that shocking.
<v M. Gessen>在同样的几十年里，就像任何家庭一样，人们做过糟糕的决定，说过希望谁也不要记住的话，对彼此发过火、记过仇、最终又和好如初，这个家庭总能在需要时做出调整。然后，它突然断裂了。有人做出了一件极其恶劣、极其令人震惊的事。

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:31.240
<v M. Gessen>That person was my cousin, Allen. He and his mother, my father's sister, Lenna, came to the US from Moscow in 1990, when Allen was 15. They stayed with my parents and brother for almost a year. By the time they arrived, I no longer lived at home, so I didn't have much of a relationship with them. I never really wanted to because I didn't like my aunt. And as Allen grew up, I realized even from a distance that I didn't particularly like him either.
<v M. Gessen>这个人就是我的堂弟阿伦。1990年，阿伦15岁时，他和他的母亲——也就是我父亲的妹妹列娜——从莫斯科来到美国。他们在我的父母和弟弟家住了将近一年。他们到达时，我已经不在家住了，所以我跟他们的关系并不深。我其实一直不想跟他们亲近，因为我讨厌我的姑姑。随着阿伦渐渐长大，即使保持着距离，我也发现自己特别讨厌他。

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:51.680
<v M. Gessen>Allen is a clown, a blowhard, a pompous ass. He would call himself an entrepreneur. He started his first business in college. He hired students to ghostwrite papers for other, wealthier students. He went to law school and got fired from his first job. He later told me this was because his fine legal mind made the other lawyers insecure.
<v M. Gessen>阿伦是个小丑、吹牛大王、自命不凡的蠢货。他会自称为企业家。他在读大学时创办了第一家公司，雇佣学生为其他更富有的学生代写论文。他读了法学院，结果在第一份工作时就被解雇了。他后来告诉我，这是因为他高超的法律头脑让其他律师感到了不安全。

00:03:51.680 --> 00:04:09.920
<v M. Gessen>Then he lived in Russia, Ukraine, Zimbabwe, working a series of increasingly shady jobs. In Africa, he was involved with diamonds and worked with an Israeli company that provided security for mining. If someone had set out to write an unlikeable international huckster character, they couldn't have laid it on any thicker.
<v M. Gessen>后来他住在俄罗斯、乌克兰、津巴布韦，做着一份又一份越来越可疑的工作。在非洲，他涉足钻石业务，并为一家为采矿提供安保的以色列公司工作。如果有人故意要写一个讨人厌的国际骗子角色，也绝对无法把他写得更让人讨厌了。

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:34.600
<v M. Gessen>Allen married a Zimbabwean woman. Word in the family was that she had been that country's beauty queen. They had two kids. Last I knew, all of them, including my aunt Lenna, were living in Moscow. And then in the summer of 2019, everyone on the American side of the family got a Facebook message from Allen, informing us that he had arrived in the US with his five-year-old son, who I'm going to call O.
<v M. Gessen>所以，我不知道该怎么说，但确实有一种更便宜、可能也更彻底的方法来解决这事，不过——

00:04:34.600 --> 00:05:00.600
<v M. Gessen>Allen wrote they'd come for O to quote, "commence his studies." I repeat, O was five. His wife, he wrote, was still in Russia with their baby daughter. They had separated. Allen added ominously, quote, "things are less than amicable. She might make attempts to contact you with requests detrimental to mine and O's interests," unquote.
<v M. Gessen>阿伦写道，他们来是为了让O“开始他的学业”。我重申一遍，O当时五岁。他写道，他的妻子和他们的女儿仍留在俄罗斯。他们分居了。阿伦不祥地补充道：“情况不太友好。她可能会试图联系你们，提出损害我和O利益的要求。”

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:21.960
<v M. Gessen>I immediately texted my brother Keith, who was closer to Allen. So our cousin has kidnapped his son and abandoned his daughter? "The answer would appear to be maybe," my brother responded. Just a note, this isn't the big shocking thing I was talking about earlier. We're still a few years away from that.
<v M. Gessen>我立刻给跟我关系更近的弟弟基思发了短信。所以我们的堂弟绑架了他的儿子，抛弃了女儿？“答案似乎是‘有可能’，”我弟弟回复道。顺便说明一下，这还不是我刚才说的那个极其震惊的大事件。离那件事我们还有好几年。

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:56.480
<v M. Gessen>I called my dad. He told me that Allen had just shown up at his house on Cape Cod without warning. His five-year-old son was with him, as was Lenna, my dad's sister. I asked my dad if we should do something about the maybe kidnapping. Like, I don't know, contact the FBI? This was the wrong thing to say to a guy who grew up in the Soviet Union. He would never call the authorities on his sister and nephew. What he did do was post a picture of O on Facebook, perhaps a message in a bottle for O's mom. Sure enough, my father immediately heard from her.
<v M. Gessen>我给爸爸打了电话。他告诉我，阿伦毫无预兆地突然出现在他位于科德角的房子里。他五岁的儿子和他在一起，列娜——我爸爸的妹妹——也在一起。我问爸爸，我们是不是该对这起“可能发生的绑架”做点什么？比如说，联系联邦调查局什么的？对一个在苏联长大的人说这话，可就大错特错了。他绝不可能为了自己的妹妹和侄子去报警。他实际做的是在脸书上发布了一张O的照片，这或许就像是给O的妈妈发出的一封漂流瓶信件。果不其然，我父亲立刻收到了她的回复。

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:14.600
<v M. Gessen>Her name is Priscilla. Priscilla wrote to my dad describing the ordeal she was enduring. She said she had gone on a short business trip to Zimbabwe, and when she returned, she discovered that Allen had left with their son. It had been about a week, and only now from seeing my father's Facebook post, was she learning anything more.
<v M. Gessen>她的名字叫普莉希拉。普莉希拉写信给我的父亲，描述了她正在经受的折磨。她说自己去津巴布韦出了一趟短差，等她回来时，发现阿伦已经带着他们的儿子离开了。大约已经过去了一个星期，直到现在看到我父亲在脸书上发的照片，她才得知了更多消息。

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:26.640
<v M. Gessen>Priscilla wrote, "I beg you, please to help me get my son back, or to at least speak to him. Please do not tell them I have written to you. If you are unable to help me, then just ignore my message."
<v M. Gessen>普莉希拉写道：“我求求你，请帮我把儿子找回来，或者至少让我跟他通个电话。请不要告诉他们我给你写了信。如果你帮不上忙，那就请无视我的信息。”

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:31.920
<v Alex>I received a long, long letter from Priscilla, but I just ignored it.
<v Alex>我收到了普莉希拉写的好长好长的信，但我直接无视了。

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:34.400
<v M. Gessen>My father can be quite literal.
<v M. Gessen>我父亲这个人有时非常死板。

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:40.960
<v M. Gessen>So what did you think was going on then? Did you think she was lying or--
<v M. Gessen>那你当时觉得是怎么回事？你觉得她在撒谎还是——

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:52.520
<v Alex>Honestly, I didn't pay much attention. I don't know. No. I understood that something was wrong with their marriage. But beyond that, no.
<v Alex>老实说，我没太在意。我不知道。没有。我理解他们的婚姻出了问题。但除此之外，没有了。

00:06:52.520 --> 00:07:12.520
<v M. Gessen>Like I said, my family's elastic. To keep it that way, my father preferred not to know too much. And it wasn't just him. My three younger brothers, their partners, my own grown son, assorted friends of my father's, everyone acted like, hey, sometimes men and their mothers just change continents with a five-year-old in tow.
<v M. Gessen>就像我说的，我的家庭很有弹性。为了保持这种状态，我父亲选择知道得越少越好。不只是他，我的三个弟弟、他们的伴侣、我成年不久的儿子，以及我父亲的各种朋友，每个人都表现得好像——嘿，有时男人和他们的母亲带着一个五岁小孩换个大陆生活不是很正常吗？

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:34.800
<v M. Gessen>And here's the thing. They were fun. My father loves having family around. The whole reason he lives in a big house on Cape Cod is so that his four kids and five grandkids gather around him. But the house has seen better days. And all the kids and some of the grandkids have busy lives. Allen and Lenna and O's arrival on the scene breathed new life into the house and the family.
<v M. Gessen>关键是，他们很有趣。我父亲喜欢家里热热闹闹的。他住在科德角大房子的全部原因，就是为了让四个孩子和五个孙辈聚在自己身边。但这栋房子已经风光不再。所有的孩子和部分孙辈都有着忙碌的生活。阿伦、列娜和O的到来，为这栋房子和这个家庭注入了新的活力。

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:50.320
<v M. Gessen>Lenna would come up with ridiculous activities like let's write the Gessen family anthem, and was always taking black and white pictures that made us all look like more stylish versions of ourselves. Allen was always driving up in his Tesla with new gadgets and tales of new business ventures.
<v M. Gessen>列娜会想出一些荒谬的活动，比如让我们写一首盖森家族的颂歌，她还总是拍一些黑白照片，让我们看起来都成了自己更具时尚感的版本。阿伦则总是开着他的特斯拉带着新奇的小玩意和新的商业风投故事驶来。

00:07:50.320 --> 00:08:11.360
<v M. Gessen>I found him ridiculous, but my youngest brothers and my oldest son hung on every word. Allen would sit on the couch with these very young men and scroll through pictures of women on Tinder. They all looked like models. Allen was bald as a billiard ball and had a giant protruding belly. He claimed that he had matched with all of those women.
<v M. Gessen>我觉得他荒谬透顶，但我最小的弟弟和大儿子却对他的每一句话深信不疑。阿伦会和这些非常年轻的小伙子们一起坐在沙发上，滑动着查看Tinder上的女性照片。她们看起来都像模特。阿伦秃得像个台球，还挺着个巨大的肚子。他声称自己和所有这些女性都配对成功了。

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:27.320
<v M. Gessen>After a while, Allen was eager to talk about why he had taken O. He claimed that Priscilla was a bad mother. She partied all the time. She did drugs. She cheated on Allen. To me, these sounded like good reasons to get a divorce, not to take your child from his mother.
<v M. Gessen>过了一段时间，阿伦迫不及待地开始谈论他为什么要带走O。他声称普莉希拉是个糟糕的母亲。她整天聚会。她吸毒。她背叛了阿伦。在我看来，这些充其量是离婚的好理由，而不是把孩子从母亲身边带走的理由。

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:51.520
<v M. Gessen>Lenna had her own complaints. She said Priscilla didn't read to her child, and perhaps even worse, didn't read books herself. The only book she kept in the house, Lenna claimed, was the Bible. I thought, wait, this was why Lenna and Allen took Priscilla's son away? There are few things that I think justify separating a kid from his parent, but Lenna and Allen didn't seem to think that much justification was required.
<v M. Gessen>列娜也有她自己的抱怨。她说普莉希拉不给孩子读书，甚至更糟的是，她自己根本不读书。列娜声称，家里唯一的一本书就是《圣经》。我想，等等，这就是列娜和阿伦把普莉希拉的儿子带走的理由？我觉得有什么理由能正当化把孩子和父母强行分开的情况少之又少，但列娜和阿伦似乎觉得根本不需要什么充分的理由。

00:08:51.520 --> 00:09:18.840
<v M. Gessen>I couldn't stop thinking about what Priscilla must be going through. Without telling anyone in the family, I decided to reach out to her. I had met her only a couple of times and barely had a sense of her. I knew that she worked in fashion. I knew from Lenna that Priscilla's father owned a huge farm in Zimbabwe. And I knew that she would have no reason to trust me. I wasn't sure she'd respond. I texted her that I knew only Lenna and Allen's side of the story.
<v M. Gessen>我脑子里一直想着普莉希拉正在经历什么。在没有告诉家里任何人的情况下，我决定联系她。我只见过她几次，对她几乎没有什么了解。我知道她从事时尚行业。我从列娜那里得知，普莉希拉的父亲在津巴布韦拥有一座大农场。我也知道她没有任何理由信任我。我不确定她是否会回复。我给她发短信，说我只知道列娜和阿伦这一方的说法。

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:36.680
<v M. Gessen>Priscilla wrote back right away. She was stuck in Russia. Her daughter, whom I'll call L, had been born via surrogacy because Priscilla was unable to carry a pregnancy to term. The baby was eight months old, but Priscilla still didn't have a birth certificate for her, which meant that they couldn't leave the country.
<v M. Gessen>普莉希拉立刻回了信。她被困在了俄罗斯。她的女儿（我称她为L）是通过代孕出生的，因为普莉希拉无法正常怀孕。孩子八个月大了，但普莉希拉仍然没有给她办出生证明，这意味着他们无法离开这个国家。

00:09:36.680 --> 00:10:00.850
<v M. Gessen>We traded short messages back and forth. Our exchange was friendly, but guarded. I didn't want to overstep, and I think Priscilla tried to say only what needed to be said. It was enough for me to sense that she was in anguish, and I was horrified. How could this woman's child just be taken away from her? How could my family just sit by? And what was going to happen to O now?
<v M. Gessen>我们来来回回发了简短的短信。我们的交流很友好，但有所保留。我不想越界，我想普莉希拉也尽量只说必要的话。这足以让我感觉到她处于极度的痛苦之中，我感到震惊。这个女人的孩子怎么能就这样被夺走？我的家人怎么能坐视不管？O现在会怎么样？

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:19.330
<v M. Gessen>Priscilla told me that the Russian police would not help her. The Zimbabwean embassy said that she could file a petition under the Hague Convention, a treaty that specifically addresses situations when one parent abducts a child and takes them to another country. But Priscilla needed legal help in the US. I could be useful here.
<v M. Gessen>普莉希拉告诉我，俄罗斯警方不会帮她。津巴布韦大使馆表示，她可以根据《海牙公约》提起诉讼，这是一项专门针对父母一方绑架儿童并将其带到另一个国家的条约。但普莉希拉需要美国的法律援助。我在这里可以派上用场。

00:10:19.330 --> 00:10:39.650
<v M. Gessen>I called a friend who connected Priscilla with a person in the Justice Department who specializes in these kinds of cases. Priscilla also needed Lenna, Allen, and O's physical address in the States so she could begin the Hague process. This I could definitely help with. I knew that they'd left Cape Cod for New York, which is where I live.
<v M. Gessen>我给一个朋友打了电话，他把普莉希拉和司法部一位专门处理此类案件的人联系上了。普莉希拉还需要列娜、阿伦和O在美国的实际住址，以便启动海牙公约程序。这我绝对能帮上忙。我知道他们已经从科德角搬到了纽约，也就是我住的地方。

00:10:39.650 --> 00:11:01.970
<v M. Gessen>I invited my aunt, cousin, and nephew over for dinner. Allen was away on business, so Lenna arrived with O, who got conscripted into a human pyramid by the young people of my household. As I slid turkey steaks into the oven, I asked Lenna the question all New York City parents ask all other New York City parents, where will O go to school? He was about to turn six.
<v M. Gessen>我邀请了我的姑姑、堂弟和侄子来家里吃晚饭。阿伦外出出差了，所以列娜带着O一起来的，O被我家里的年轻人拉去搭人肉金字塔。我把火烤牛排推入烤箱时，向列娜问了所有纽约市父母都会问所有其他纽约市父母的问题：O要去哪里上学？他快六岁了。

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:26.450
<v M. Gessen>Lenna said that she had no idea how schools even functioned in the city. Do let me explain this to you, I said, and took out my phone. What is your address? Let's see what district that is. Bingo. I had their address. I sent it to Priscilla. Some weeks later, apparently on a lark, they moved to Massachusetts. I figured out that address, too.
<v M. Gessen>列娜说她根本不知道学校在城里是怎么运作的。我来跟你解释一下吧，我说道，并掏出了手机。你们的地址是什么？让我们看看那是哪个学区。太棒了。我得到了他们的地址。我把它发给了普莉希拉。几个星期后，显然是一时兴起，他们搬到了马萨诸塞州。我也弄清了那个地址。

00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:45.170
<v M. Gessen>I was a double agent now. I tracked Lenna, Allen, and O through their Facebook posts, messages to the family chat, and occasional weekends at my father's house on Cape Cod. When they moved to a new house, I let Priscilla know. If I had news about O, I texted Priscilla. Sometimes she'd just ask for reassurance that he was all right.
<v M. Gessen>我现在是个双重间谍了。我通过列娜、阿伦和O的脸书动态、家庭群聊信息以及偶尔在科德角我父亲家的周末来追踪他们的行踪。当他们搬进新家时，我会通知普莉希拉。如果我有关于O的消息，我就会给普莉希拉发短信。有时她只是要求得到安慰，确认他一切都好。

00:11:45.170 --> 00:12:05.250
<v M. Gessen>From all the men in my family, my father, my three brothers, and my son, I hid the fact that I was in touch with Priscilla. I thought they'd see what I was doing as disloyal and might rat me out to Allen. My daughter knew. It was a little bit exciting, but it also gave me an excuse for maintaining peace with my newly enlarged family.
<v M. Gessen>我向家里所有的男性——我的父亲、三个弟弟和我的儿子——隐瞒了我和普莉希拉保持联系的事。我想他们会认为我的所作所为是不忠的，并且可能会向阿伦告发我。我的女儿知道。这稍微有点刺激，但也为我和我新扩大的家庭维持和平提供了一个借口。

00:12:05.250 --> 00:12:34.410
<v M. Gessen>But the more I hung out with them, the more I just hung out with them. O was growing. Allen and Lenna were building a life. I watched. Sometimes I caught myself thinking that it was a pretty good life. Allen, Lenna, and O moved into a farmhouse in Concord, Massachusetts. Lenna furnished it stylishly. They seemed to spend most of their time actively raising O. They enrolled him in Jewish school, violin lessons, fencing, horseback riding. And I'm sure I'm still forgetting something.
<v M. Gessen>但我和他们待得越久，就真的只是和他们待在一起。O在长高。阿伦和列娜在构建生活。我看着。有时我甚至发现自己觉得这过得还挺不错。阿伦、列娜和O搬进了马萨诸塞州康科德的一座农舍。列娜把那里布置得很时尚。他们的大部分时间似乎都花在积极抚育O上。他们给他报名了犹太学校、小提琴课、击剑课、骑马课。我确信我还漏掉了一些。

00:12:34.410 --> 00:13:10.490
<v M. Gessen>They dressed O like a tiny little gentleman, complete with brogues and fedora hats. And by some sort of miracle, the result wasn't annoying. O was a delight, curious, entertaining without being overbearing, and unfailingly polite. He seemed happy. Whatever damage being separated from his mother had done, I couldn't see it. What I could see was that he was doted on and thriving. To put it another way, and it wasn't easy for me to admit that I was seeing this, Allen seemed like a great dad, kind, attentive, devoted, and fun.
<v M. Gessen>他们把O打扮成一个小绅士，穿着雕花皮鞋，戴着呢帽。不知怎么的，结果竟然一点也不让人厌烦。O非常招人喜欢，既好奇又懂事，既不傲慢又始终温和有礼。他看起来很快乐。与母亲分离所造成的任何伤害，我都看不出来。我能看到的是，他备受宠爱且茁壮成长。换句话说——要承认我看到了这一点对我来说并不容易——阿伦看起来是个好爸爸，善良、体贴、专一且有趣。

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:33.690
<v M. Gessen>Two years passed like this. Eventually, Priscilla and L, who was now a toddler, made it to the United States. I hadn't messaged with Priscilla in over a year, but I heard from my father that Priscilla's claim, filed under the Hague Convention, was going to be heard in federal court in Boston. The case would probably drag on for a while, but I assumed that Priscilla would now be able to see her son.
<v M. Gessen>两年就这样过去了。最终，普莉希拉和现在已经是个幼儿的L成功来到了美国。我有一年多没和普莉希拉发过短信了，但我从父亲那里听说，普莉希拉根据《海牙公约》提起的诉讼将在波士顿的联邦法院开庭审理。这个案子可能会拖延一段时间，但我认为普莉希拉现在终于可以见到她的儿子了。

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:48.970
<v M. Gessen>And then there it was on social media. Priscilla posted a picture of herself embracing O. I liked the picture. I figured my job was done, my time as a double agent long over.
<v M. Gessen>然后它就出现在了社交媒体上。普莉希拉发布了一张她拥抱O的照片。我给这张照片点了赞。我想我的任务完成了，我作为双重间谍的时期早就结束了。

00:13:48.970 --> 00:13:57.450
<v M. Gessen>About four months later, Allen was arrested for kidnapping O. Not for the time he took O from Russia. This was new.
<v M. Gessen>大约四个月后，阿伦因绑架O被捕。不是因为他从俄罗斯带走O的那次。这是新的。

00:13:57.450 --> 00:14:09.250
<v Ira Glass>That incident, which I need to say, is still not the big shocking thing that rocked Masha's family. That's coming up. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>那起事件——我必须说，依然不是震撼玛莎一家的那个惊天大事件。那件事马上就来。请继续收听。

00:14:09.250 --> 00:14:17.210
<v Ira Glass>This is American Life. Let's just pick up with M. Gessen's story where we left off-- Allen taking O a second time.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《此生美国》。让我们从玛莎·盖森故事中断的地方继续——阿伦第二次带走了O。

00:14:17.210 --> 00:14:34.890
<v M. Gessen>Allen was arrested in Montreal at the airport when he, Lenna, and O were waiting to board a flight to London without, apparently, Priscilla's knowledge. This time Allen went to jail. But no, this arrest and what Allen did to get himself arrested weren't the things that shocked my family.
<v M. Gessen>艾伦在蒙特利尔机场被捕，当时他和琳娜以及O正准备登机飞往伦敦，显然普莉希拉对此并不知情。这一次，艾伦进了监狱。但是，这次被捕以及艾伦为让自己被捕所做的事，并不是最让我家人震惊的地方。

00:14:34.890 --> 00:14:54.970
<v M. Gessen>We didn't exactly act like Allen's arrest was normal. We acted like it was absurd. I entertained my friends with stories of my serial kidnapper cousin. Lenna kept the family updated with over-dramatic notes on the Facebook family chat, and at least one video from Canada in which Allen, wearing a striped uniform, sings her a Russian prison song.
<v M. Gessen>我们并没有表现得好像艾伦被捕是一件正常的事。我们的反应是觉得荒谬绝伦。我用我连环绑架犯表弟的故事来取悦朋友。琳娜则在脸书家族群里不断更新那些充满夸张戏剧色彩的文字，并分享了至少一段来自加拿大的视频，视频中穿着条纹囚服的艾伦为她唱了一首俄罗斯监狱歌曲。

00:14:54.970 --> 00:14:57.130
<v M. Gessen>[RUSSIAN SINGING]
<v M. Gessen>[俄罗斯歌曲歌唱]

00:14:57.130 --> 00:15:01.690
<v M. Gessen>It looked like a cartoon.
<v M. Gessen>这看起来就像卡通片一样。

00:15:01.690 --> 00:15:16.090
<v M. Gessen>Allen spent a couple of weeks in Canadian detention, then another few weeks in a jail in upstate New York, and was finally released on his own recognizance to await trial in Massachusetts. O was now living with Priscilla.
<v M. Gessen>艾伦在加拿大的羁押所度过了几周，接着又在纽约州北部的一所监狱里待了几周，最后在马萨诸塞州获准取保候审以等待审判。此时，O已经和普莉希拉住在一起了。

00:15:16.090 --> 00:15:38.410
<v M. Gessen>Allen got out of jail in February 2022. A couple of months after that, he sent out a missive on the family chat, as self-important as the one that began this whole story. This time, he was telling us that he and Priscilla had resolved their battle, which actually turned out to be true. They would now have shared custody of both kids. Allen said he was very pleased.
<v M. Gessen>艾伦于2022年2月出狱。几个月后，他在家族群里发了一条长信，其自命不凡的程度不亚于开启整个故事的那一条。这一次，他和我们说他和普莉希拉已经化解了他们的矛盾，而这后来证明居然是真的。他们现在将共同拥有两个孩子的抚养权。艾伦说他非常高兴。

00:15:38.410 --> 00:16:01.890
<v M. Gessen>I thought, my God, did you have to go through all this, absconding with your son twice, keeping him separated from his mother for more than two years, just to arrive at a standard 50/50 custody agreement? This, child support and shared custody, is the boring end of this crazy story? I felt a little relieved and a little dumb, like maybe I bought too fully into other people's drama.
<v M. Gessen>我想，我的天啊，难道你们非得经历这一切——两次拐走儿子，让他和母亲分离两年多——就为了达成一个标准的一半对一半的抚养权协议吗？这就是这个疯狂故事的无聊结局——抚养费和共同抚养权？我感到既有点如释重负，又有点像个傻瓜，仿佛我太深地卷入了别人的戏剧之中。

00:16:01.890 --> 00:16:17.970
<v M. Gessen>Kidnapping charges against Allen were pending. They would later be dropped. And still, Priscilla was able to reach a peace agreement with Allen, after all he'd apparently put her and their son through. Well, maybe this was just the way they did things, with extreme flair.
<v M. Gessen>针对艾伦的绑架指控原本处于未决状态。后来这些指控被撤销了。即便如此，普莉希拉还是能够与艾伦达成和平协议，尽管艾伦显然曾让她和他们的儿子经历过那么多磨难。好吧，也许这就是他们做事的方式，充满了极致的戏剧性。

00:16:17.970 --> 00:16:26.850
<v Alex>Then, yeah, a kind of exotic part started.
<v Alex>然后，没错，某种富有异国情调的部分开始了。

00:16:26.850 --> 00:16:33.290
<v M. Gessen>Then it happened. The thing, the bomb that went off in the middle of my family.
<v M. Gessen>然后事情就发生了。那件事，就像在我家里引爆的一颗炸弹。

00:16:33.290 --> 00:16:45.090
<v Alex>So the day before, Allen called me and said that he promised his kids to take them camping.
<v Alex>就在前一天，艾伦给我打电话说，他答应了孩子们带他们去露营。

00:16:45.090 --> 00:16:56.770
<v M. Gessen>July 2022. Under the new custody arrangement, it was Allen's weekend with the kids. He asked my dad, hey, do you mind if me, my mom, and the kids camp out in your backyard on Cape Cod?
<v M. Gessen>2022年7月。根据新的抚养权安排，这个周末轮到艾伦陪伴孩子。他问我爸爸：“嘿，我和我妈还有孩子们能去你在科德角的后院露营吗，你介意吗？”

00:16:56.770 --> 00:17:10.690
<v Alex>I said, of course. So they came. They brought some huge, huge tent. I never saw such a tent before, with a lot of furniture, and lights, and devices.
<v Alex>我说，当然可以。于是他们来了。他们带来了一个超级巨大的帐篷。我以前从未见过这样的帐篷，里面有许多家具、灯光和各种设备。

00:17:10.690 --> 00:17:29.850
<v M. Gessen>Solar chargers, rugs, two full mattresses, a treasure trunk with, uh, treasures, I guess? It was very Allen-- awesome, spectacular, ridiculous. Though later it occurred to me that this time, at least, there may have been a point to this. He wanted everyone to remember his camping trip to my father's backyard.
<v M. Gessen>太阳能充电器、地毯、两张全尺寸床垫，还有一个装着……嗯，我猜是宝藏的宝箱？这非常符合艾伦的作风——酷炫、壮观、荒谬。不过后来我突然想到，至少这一次，他这么做可能是有目的的。他想让所有人都记住这次在我父亲后院的露营之旅。

00:17:29.850 --> 00:17:52.090
<v M. Gessen>Because it was summer, my father's house was full. Two of my younger brothers, one of them with his girlfriend, were there. Everyone had a nice dinner together and then went to bed, some people in the house, and Allen, Lenna, and the kids in the tent. And then around 6:00 the next morning, the dog, Alton, started going nuts. Someone was banging on the front door.
<v M. Gessen>因为正值夏天，父亲的房子里住满了人。我的两个弟弟，其中一个还带着女朋友，都在那里。大家一起享用了美好的晚餐，然后就去睡觉了，有些人睡在屋里，艾伦、琳娜和孩子们则睡在帐篷里。接着大约第二天早上6点，那只名叫奥尔顿的狗开始疯狂狂吠。有人在猛烈敲打前门。

00:17:52.090 --> 00:18:10.890
<v Alex>So I opened the door a bit because not to let Alton out. Also, I didn't put my trousers on yet. And the guy, the policeman said, we are state police. Could you step out with your phone?
<v Alex>于是我把门开了一条缝，因为不想让奥尔顿跑出去。而且，我当时还没穿好裤子。那个警察说，我们是州警察。你能拿着手机出来一下吗？

00:18:10.890 --> 00:18:15.730
<v M. Gessen>My dad is surprised, but he's not panicking. He goes to get his pants and his phone.
<v M. Gessen>我父亲很惊讶，但他并没有惊慌。他去拿了他的裤子和手机。

00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:25.410
<v Alex>But by that time, because of all this noise and commotion and Alton's barking, Allosha woke up.
<v Alex>但就在那时，由于这所有的噪音、骚动以及奥尔顿的狂吠，阿洛沙醒了。

00:18:25.410 --> 00:18:27.960
<v M. Gessen>Allosha is my cousin's Russian diminutive.
<v M. Gessen>阿洛沙是我表弟名字的俄罗斯语爱称。

00:18:27.960 --> 00:18:39.690
<v Alex>Allen, and he came to the house to see what is going on. And the police figured out that they are looking for him and not for me.
<v Alex>艾伦，他来到屋里查看发生了什么。警察这才发现他们要找的是他，而不是我。

00:18:39.690 --> 00:18:56.650
<v M. Gessen>FBI agents go around the house, banging on doors, and make everyone sit down on the couches in the living room. No one understands what's going on. But soon, through the picture windows that look out on the backyard, they see two male FBI agents take Allen away in handcuffs.
<v M. Gessen>联邦调查局特工在房子四周巡视，猛力敲门，并让所有人都坐在起居室的沙发上。没有人明白发生了什么。但很快，透过望向后院的落地窗，他们看到两名男联邦调查局特工给艾伦戴上手铐，把他带走了。

00:18:56.650 --> 00:19:05.410
<v M. Gessen>Then a female agent escorts the kids to another car. They all drive off. State troopers follow. Lenna leaves too.
<v M. Gessen>接着一名女特工护送孩子们上另一辆车。他们都开车离去了。州警紧随其后。琳娜也离开了。

00:19:05.410 --> 00:19:12.610
<v M. Gessen>And did you know, once everybody left, did you have any idea what he had been arrested for?
<v M. Gessen>你知道吗，一旦大家都离开后，你有没有任何头绪他究竟是因为什么被捕的？

00:19:12.610 --> 00:19:31.650
<v Alex>Not immediately, but then I learned from Lenna about that. She was-- she was totally lost. But the only thing she knew that what was in this paper they gave her.
<v Alex>不是立刻知道的，但后来我从琳娜那里得知了。她当时——她完全不知所措。她唯一知道的就是他们给她的那张纸上写的内容。

00:19:31.650 --> 00:19:34.130
<v M. Gessen>What was in the paper?
<v M. Gessen>纸上写了什么？

00:19:34.130 --> 00:19:43.170
<v Alex>Well, that he is arrested for-- I don't remember. But murder for hire was there. Yes.
<v Alex>嗯，他是因为……我不记得了。但里面有“雇凶杀人”这几个字。是的。

00:19:43.170 --> 00:19:54.210
<v M. Gessen>And did you have any idea who he might have hired somebody to murder?
<v M. Gessen>你有没有猜到他可能雇了什么人去谋杀谁？

00:19:54.210 --> 00:19:58.410
<v Alex>Uh, it didn't take long.
<v Alex>嗯，没花多长时间就知道了。

00:19:58.410 --> 00:20:04.730
<v M. Gessen>It was Priscilla. Allen, it seemed, had hired someone to kill Priscilla.
<v M. Gessen>是普莉希拉。看样子，艾伦雇人去谋杀普莉希拉。

00:20:04.730 --> 00:20:10.930
<v Alex>The question was if it was true or not. That's another story.
<v Alex>问题在于这是真是假。那是另一个故事了。

00:20:10.930 --> 00:20:37.810
<v M. Gessen>Some of us took the news in faster than others. The day after Allen's arrest, my brother Keith and I had a fight over the Justice Department press release, which identified the target only as PC. I was saying that it was obviously Priscilla, whose last name begins with a C. He was saying that it was obviously not Priscilla. Lenna kept telling everyone that Allen had been set up by business rivals, or Russian agents, or the FBI, or someone.
<v M. Gessen>我们当中有些人接受这个消息的速度比其他人更快。在艾伦被捕的第二天，我和我弟弟基思为了司法部的通告吵了一架，通告中将目标仅标识为PC。我说这显然是普莉希拉（Priscilla），她的姓氏以C开头。他说这显然不是普莉希拉。琳娜则一直对所有人说，艾伦是被商业竞争对手、俄罗斯特工、联邦调查局或者别的什么人陷害的。

00:20:37.810 --> 00:21:05.810
<v M. Gessen>But over the course of a few days, it sank in. My cousin had been caught hiring someone to murder his ex-wife, the mother of his children. This was when it felt like we snapped. I certainly snapped. I was shocked at how shocked I was. It's not that I felt bad for Allen or Lenna. It's just, how does something like this happen? How had it happened right here in my family, in between our silly dinners, and chess games, and kids' birthday parties?
<v M. Gessen>但在几天的时间里，这个事实沉甸甸地压在了我们心头。我的表弟竟然雇人谋杀他的前妻、他孩子们的母亲，这件事败露了。就在那时，我们感觉自己崩溃了。我确实崩溃了。我对自己的震惊程度感到震惊。倒不是说我为艾伦或琳娜感到难过，而是，怎么会发生这种事？它怎么会就发生在我的家族里，在我们那些愚蠢的晚餐、下棋局和孩子们的生日派对之间？

00:21:05.810 --> 00:21:23.210
<v M. Gessen>In theory, I knew that this kind of thing can happen in any family. Anyone's first cousin could be plotting murder. Upstanding citizens are always turning out to be secret criminals, and I wouldn't even call Allen an upstanding citizen. But it's one thing to know and another thing to understand.
<v M. Gessen>从理论上讲，我知道这种事可能会发生在任何家庭里。任何人的堂兄弟都可能在策划谋杀。正派公民总是不断被发现是秘密罪犯，而我甚至都不会称艾伦为正派公民。但知道是一回事，理解又是另一回事。

00:21:23.210 --> 00:22:01.850
<v M. Gessen>I'm a reporter. At some of the hardest times in my life, when I faced a dire medical diagnosis, I put on my reporter's hat and asked everyone a lot of questions. It has allowed me to wrap my mind around unthinkable things before. Allen was in jail awaiting trial, so my project had to begin with Priscilla, who was thankfully alive. What she told me was so much worse than what I thought I knew. That's next time. From Serial Productions and The New York Times I'm M. Gessen, and this is The Idiot.
<v M. Gessen>我是一名记者。在我人生的某些最艰难时刻，当我面对可怕的疾病诊断时，我戴上记者的帽子，向所有人提出大量问题。这曾让我能够坦然面对以前不可想象的事情。艾伦当时身在狱中等待审判，所以我的调查项目必须从普莉希拉开始，谢天谢地她还活着。她告诉我的一切，比我以为自己知道的要糟糕得多。下期节目再见。我是M·盖森，来自Serial Productions和《纽约时报》，这里是《白痴》。

00:22:01.850 --> 00:22:09.170
<v Ira Glass>OK, so that is the first episode of your new podcast, The Idiot. Does Allen know the name of the show yet?
<v Ira Glass>好的，这就是你的新播客《白痴》的第一集。艾伦现在知道这个节目的名字了吗？

00:22:09.170 --> 00:22:21.110
<v M. Gessen>You know-- I mean, obviously there are some parts of that title that might be appealing to Allen. It's reference to a classic work of Russian literature.
<v M. Gessen>你知道的——我的意思是，很显然这个标题的某些部分可能会吸引艾伦。这是对俄罗斯文学经典著作的致敬。

00:22:21.110 --> 00:22:21.810
<v Ira Glass>Dostoyevsky.
<v Ira Glass>陀思妥耶夫斯基。

00:22:21.810 --> 00:23:02.450
<v M. Gessen>Dostoyevsky's novel, The Idiot. And I think there's a little bit of kindness in that title. I think that I'm giving him the grace of perceiving what he did is just an incredibly dumb thing, and not only a very scary, mean, and evil thing. And also, he's very lucky that he was bad enough at trying to hire a killer that everyone, in the end, is alive, and he's serving only a 10-year sentence.
<v M. Gessen>陀思妥耶夫斯基的小说《白痴》。而且我认为这个标题里包含着一丝善意。我觉得我赋予了他一种体谅，即把他的所作所为视为一件极其愚蠢的事，而不仅仅是一件非常可怕、卑鄙和邪恶的事。而且，他也非常幸运，他在试图雇凶杀人这方面笨手笨脚，以至于到头来所有人都还活着，而他只需服刑10年。

00:23:02.450 --> 00:23:12.410
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. So after that, you began reporting, and as you say at the end of episode one, you start with Priscilla. What happens?
<v Ira Glass>是的。所以在那之后，你开始进行报道，正如你在第一集结尾所说的那样，你从普莉希拉开始。发生了什么？

00:23:12.410 --> 00:23:45.050
<v M. Gessen>I'd only met Priscilla a couple of times in my life. I didn't know her. I just knew she was this sort of beautiful, poised woman who'd been through hell at this point and had come to the US to try to get custody back of her child. But I didn't know how the story had unfolded for her. So let me play an excerpt from that conversation. I started with something that had mystified me for a long time.
<v M. Gessen>我一生中只见过普莉希拉几次。我不了解她。我只知道她是一个美丽、沉着稳重的女人，当时已经历了人间炼狱，并来到美国试图夺回孩子的抚养权。但我不知道这个故事对她而言是如何展开的。所以让我播放一段我们那次对话的节子。我从一个让我困惑了很久的问题开始。

00:23:45.050 --> 00:23:51.490
<v M. Gessen>So can you tell me what you saw in Allen when you first met him?
<v M. Gessen>那你能不能告诉我，你第一次见到艾伦时，在他身上看到了什么？

00:23:51.490 --> 00:23:59.190
<v Priscilla>[LAUGHS] Wow. I think, like most people that meet him, the first time you meet him, he's very charismatic.
<v Priscilla>[笑] 哇。我想，就像大多数见到他的人一样，你第一次见到他时，会觉得他极具个人魅力。

00:23:59.190 --> 00:24:13.970
<v M. Gessen>This was 2011 at a party in Harare, the capital of Zimbabwe. Allen was there on business, scoping out investment opportunities for Ukrainian oligarchs. He was hustling. As my son described him once, he was an egg who knows how to talk to people.
<v M. Gessen>那是2011年，在津巴布韦首都哈拉雷的一个派对上。艾伦当时在出差，为乌克兰寡头物色投资机会。他一直在四处奔忙。正如我儿子曾对他的评价那样，他是一个懂得如何与人交流的“聪明蛋”。

00:24:13.970 --> 00:24:17.130
<v M. Gessen>And did that seem appealing?
<v M. Gessen>这看起来很有吸引力吗？

00:24:17.130 --> 00:24:36.130
<v Priscilla>It did. I'll be honest, I was 30 when I met him. It seemed very appealing, and it was very different from anybody that I had met. So different was interesting. He came from a very different part of the world, which I knew nothing about, which was also exciting in its own regard.
<v Priscilla>确实有。老实说，我遇到他时30岁。这看起来非常吸引人，而且与我见过的任何人都不一样。如此不同便意味着有趣。他来自一个我一无所知的世界截然不同的角落，这本身也令人兴奋。

00:24:36.130 --> 00:25:00.090
<v M. Gessen>It wasn't just exciting. It was convenient in a way. Allen was unreadable to Priscilla the way someone from Zimbabwe might be. She could project her desires onto him, including her desire for success. Priscilla was working at a new lifestyle magazine and had launched Zimbabwe's annual Fashion Week. She wanted a life that was big and fast, like Allen's. And it's true that Allen seemed to know how to make big, fast money and spend it.
<v M. Gessen>这不仅仅是令人兴奋。在某种程度上也很方便。在普莉希拉看来，艾伦是个让人捉摸不透的人，就像来自津巴布韦的人对她来说可能那样。她可以将自己的愿望投射到他身上，包括她对成功的渴望。普莉希拉当时在一家新的生活方式杂志工作，并推出了津巴布韦的年度时装周。她想要一种盛大而快节奏的生活，就像艾伦那样。而且事实也确实如此，艾伦似乎知道如何赚取巨大而快速的财富，又如何将其挥霍掉。

00:25:00.090 --> 00:25:18.170
<v Priscilla>It's like, oh, let's go to Joburg. I'm like, OK. You get up and you go, just at the drop of a hat. And then we would go here and there, and here and there. So it was very exciting. The only strange thing that happened at the beginning of our relationship when his mom came.
<v Priscilla>这就像是，哦，我们去约堡吧。我说，好的。你随时起身说走就走，说走就走。然后我们会去这儿，去那儿，去这儿，去那儿。所以非常令人兴奋。在我们关系刚开始时唯一奇怪的事情是他的妈妈来了。

00:25:18.170 --> 00:25:35.130
<v M. Gessen>Right. One of those hiccups that happen early on in a romance and should raise a giant red flag, but somehow never do. My aunt Lenna came to visit a few months into their relationship. She joined Allen and Priscilla on a trip to the countryside.
<v M. Gessen>没错。那是浪漫关系初期经常会出现的小波折之一，本该拉起巨大的红色警报，但不知为何却从没有过。在我姑妈琳娜和他们确立恋爱关系几个月后，她来探望他们。她加入了艾伦和普莉希拉的一次乡村之旅。

00:25:35.130 --> 00:26:08.970
<v Priscilla>We went on a trip to Kariba. It's a big lake in Zimbabwe. And I think it was like on the second day or something. We had a disagreement, like a fight. And he left our room and I didn't know that he had done this, but he went to his mom's room. And I found him later, I was walking past her room and she had these doors that opened out. So I just looked in and I saw him, like lying on her bed, and she was like, lying there, like stroking his hair. I found that-- well, his head.
<v Priscilla>我们去卡里巴进行了一次旅行。那是津巴布韦的一个大湖。我想大约是在第二天左右。我们产生了分歧，就像吵了一架一样。他离开了我们的房间，我不知道他做了这个举动，但他去了他妈妈的房间。我后来找到他时，我路过她的房门，她的门是向外开的。所以我朝里面看了一眼，看到他躺在她的床上，而她就那样躺在那儿，抚摸着他的头发。我觉得——嗯，摸着他的头。

00:26:08.970 --> 00:26:41.070
<v Priscilla>I found that so weird. I was like, wow, this is a grown man. And it seemed a little too intimate for me. In my culture, I guess maybe because we're very distant, you don't even hug. Like you wouldn't hug your father, because it's a little too intimate. So for an adult to be lying on his mother's bed, and for her to actually be, it just seemed very peculiar. I saw that, and I was like, OK.
<v Priscilla>我觉得这太诡异了。我想，哇，这是一个成年男子。对我来说，这似乎显得过于亲密了。在我的文化里，我想也许是因为我们的关系很疏离，你甚至都不会拥抱。比如你不会拥抱你的父亲，因为这有点过于亲密了。所以一个成年人躺在母亲的床上，而母亲居然也这么做，这看起来实在太奇怪了。我看到了那一幕，然后我想，好吧。

00:26:41.070 --> 00:26:48.770
<v Ira Glass>And as the series unfolds, Lenna and Allen's relationship is one of the things that you talk about more. Did you talk to Lenna for this story?
<v Ira Glass>随着节目的深入，琳娜和艾伦的关系成了你探讨得更多的内容之一。你为了这个故事采访过琳娜吗？

00:26:48.770 --> 00:26:52.190
<v M. Gessen>I didn't. She didn't want to talk to me.
<v M. Gessen>我没有。她不想跟我说话。

00:26:52.190 --> 00:27:06.650
<v Ira Glass>And so you're interviewing Priscilla. And the stories that she's telling you, you knew the basic plot points of-- the first time they took O, the second time they took O. What did you learn that you hadn't known?
<v Ira Glass>所以你在采访普莉希拉。她对你讲述的故事，你已经知道了基本的情节脉络——第一次他们带走O，第二次他们带走O。你了解到了什么以前不知道的事情？

00:27:06.650 --> 00:27:47.090
<v M. Gessen>So now I realize that knowing those two plot points, which were two and a half years apart, is a little bit like knowing the date the war began and the date the war ended. And like, I didn't know about all the carnage that had happened in between. At first, she was stranded in Moscow. She didn't really have any way to support herself in Moscow. She's a Zimbabwean woman who doesn't speak Russian. And that dragged on for months. And then she got back to Zimbabwe. She thought she was getting back to her regular life, from which she was going to try to make it to the US to get O back.
<v M. Gessen>所以现在我意识到，了解那两个相隔两年半的情节节点，有点像是知道战争开始的日期和战争结束的日期。而我，并不知道这期间发生的所有惨剧。起初，她被困在莫斯科。她在莫斯科没有任何维持生计的途径。她是一个不会说俄语的津巴布韦女人。这种情况持续了好几个月。然后她回到了津巴布韦。她以为自己回到了正常的生活中，并准备从那里设法去美国把O夺回来。

00:27:47.090 --> 00:28:04.270
<v M. Gessen>And then things just start happening to her in Zimbabwe. She gets beaten up by thugs. She gets picked up on drug charges. She gets picked up again and thrown into prison for two weeks. And she thinks that Allen is behind all of this. Allen denies that he had any involvement.
<v M. Gessen>接着，各种事情开始在津巴布韦降临到她头上。她遭到暴徒殴打。她因涉毒被抓。她再次被捕并被投入监狱两周。她认为这一切背后都是艾伦在搞鬼。艾伦则否认自己参与其中。

00:28:04.270 --> 00:28:09.850
<v Ira Glass>And then eventually, she goes through all of this, and she eventually gets to the United States, right?
<v Ira Glass>然后最终，她经历了这一切，最终来到了美国，对吗？

00:28:09.850 --> 00:28:29.210
<v M. Gessen>She eventually gets to the United States. It doesn't mean that she's going to get custody or even visits with her son because at this point, it's been two and a half years. But she does get to see him for the first time since he was taken from her.
<v M. Gessen>她最终来到了美国。这并不意味着她能获得抚养权，甚至都不能探望她的儿子，因为此时已经过去两年半了。但她确实见到了儿子，这是自从他被从她身边带走以来的第一次。

00:28:29.210 --> 00:28:31.650
<v Ira Glass>Wait, and so now he's how old?
<v Ira Glass>等等，那他现在多大了？

00:28:31.650 --> 00:28:35.390
<v M. Gessen>So now he is like eight years old.
<v M. Gessen>所以现在他大约八岁了。

00:28:35.390 --> 00:28:38.970
<v Ira Glass>Oh my God, so from five to eight, she hadn't seen him?
<v Ira Glass>哦，天哪，从五岁到八岁，她都没有见过他？

00:28:38.970 --> 00:28:46.010
<v M. Gessen>And also, she doesn't know what his grandmother and his father have been telling him about her.
<v M. Gessen>而且，她也不知道他的祖母和父亲一直在向他灌输关于她的什么事情。

00:28:46.010 --> 00:28:59.330
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, let's play an excerpt of this part of the episode. So this is Priscilla explaining about seeing her son for the first time after that two-year year absence.
<v Ira Glass>是的，让我们播放这集节目中这一部分的节选。这是普莉希拉在讲述在长达两年的分离后，第一次见到儿子的情景。

00:28:59.330 --> 00:29:01.610
<v M. Gessen>When did you see him for the first time?
<v M. Gessen>你是什么时候第一次见到他的？

00:29:01.610 --> 00:29:23.610
<v Priscilla>I saw him that weekend on the Sunday for the first time. It's so strange. I almost can't remember how I felt. I know I didn't cry. I couldn't cry. I think I just looked at him. I just stared at him for a while.
<v Priscilla>我在那个周末的星期天第一次见到了他。太奇怪了。我几乎记不清自己当时是什么感觉了。我知道我没有哭。我哭不出来。我想我只是看着他。我盯着他看了一会儿。

00:29:23.610 --> 00:29:28.430
<v M. Gessen>Can you describe that meeting? I mean, you had to meet outside, I think, right?
<v M. Gessen>你能描述一下那次见面吗？我的意思是，你们必须在外面见面，我想是的吧？

00:29:28.430 --> 00:30:02.540
<v Priscilla>Yeah. We met at a little tea house in the town where Allen was living, Concord. It's called Concord Tea Cakes, actually. So he was sitting outside. I saw him sitting there, and he was sitting by himself. Allen was inside the shop. When I approached him, I could actually see that he was shaking. He just seemed so small and so scared.
<v Priscilla>是的。我们在艾伦居住的城镇康科德的一家小茶馆见面。其实它叫康科德茶点屋（Concord Tea Cakes）。所以他当时坐在外面。我看到他坐在那儿，他一个人坐着。艾伦在店里面。当我走近他时，我实际上能看到他在发抖。他看起来那么弱小，那么害怕。

00:30:02.540 --> 00:30:15.420
<v M. Gessen>What had her little boy been thinking for the past two years? Why did he think his mother wasn't with him? What had Allen told him? O knew that Priscilla had been in prison. What other stories about her had taken hold in his mind?
<v M. Gessen>在过去的两年里，她的宝贝儿子一直在想些什么？为什么他会认为他的母亲没有陪在他身边？艾伦对他说过什么？O知道普莉希拉曾经入狱。关于她的其他什么传言已经在他的脑海中扎根了？

00:30:15.420 --> 00:30:37.260
<v Priscilla>And I kind of felt-- I felt helpless in a way, you know? I just said, hi. I didn't try to touch him because I could tell that he was scared. So I just said, hi. And then I just sat next to him, and I let him come to me.
<v Priscilla>我当时感觉——某种程度上我觉得很无助，你知道吗？我只是说，嗨。我没有试着去碰他，因为我看得出来他很害怕。所以我只是说，嗨。然后我就在他旁边坐下，让他自己向我走来。

00:30:37.260 --> 00:30:40.100
<v M. Gessen>Do you remember anything he said to you?
<v M. Gessen>你还记得他对你说过什么吗？

00:30:40.100 --> 00:31:23.500
<v Priscilla>He asked me for this porridge that he used to like. He had loved it since he was a baby, and he called it blue porridge. He just said to me, did you bring blue porridge? I said, yeah. They make it in Zimbabwe. And I had carried it with me. He asked me to make it for him, immediately. And I did, like in a little cup with warm water. I made it for him and he ate it. And yeah, I knew that he would slowly remember me and things would get back to where they were, if he could remember simple things like that. Yeah.
<v Priscilla>他向我要那种他以前很喜欢吃的粥。他从小就喜欢吃，他管它叫蓝色的粥。他只是对我说，你带蓝色的粥了吗？我说，带了。津巴布韦那边能做。而且我都随身带着呢。他让我马上给他做。我照做了，用一个小杯子装了点温水。我给他泡好了，他就吃了。然后，是的，我知道他会慢慢记起我，事情会回到从前的样子，如果他能记住这样简单的事情的话。是的。

00:31:23.500 --> 00:31:30.140
<v M. Gessen>That was just so heartbreaking to listen and to imagine.
<v M. Gessen>听到这些、想象当时的场景，真的太让人心碎了。

00:31:30.140 --> 00:31:39.620
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. And then you also talked about the second time Allen and Lenna take O, the one for which he was charged with kidnapping.
<v Ira Glass>是的。然后你们还谈到了艾伦和莱娜第二次带走O的那次事件，也就是他被指控绑架的那次。

00:31:39.620 --> 00:32:07.820
<v M. Gessen>Yeah. So this is the scene at the Montreal airport where they think they're going to board a flight to London, and instead Allen gets arrested. And it had been reduced to this ridiculous story that Lenna told in this over-the-top way. And I would quote from her wacky Facebook messages-- to close friends.
<v M. Gessen>对。所以这是在蒙特利尔机场的场景，当时他们以为自己要登机去伦敦，结果艾伦被捕了。这件事后来被简化成了一个荒谬的故事，是莱娜用那种极其夸张的方式讲出来的。我还会引用她发给亲密朋友的那些古怪的Facebook消息——

00:32:07.820 --> 00:32:09.460
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:32:09.460 --> 00:32:47.540
<v M. Gessen>And hearing the story from Priscilla's perspective, which is really O's perspective, just how absolutely terrifying it was for him. He's a little boy. That's his dad, who gets tackled by several armed, uniformed men and thrown to the ground. He gets dragged off. O gets taken into foster care for two days before Priscilla can come and pick him up. And again, she's separated from him. Like, it's the distance. It's the International border. It's just the pain of it is kind of unbearable.
<v M. Gessen>而从普莉希ラの角度——也就是O的角度——听到这个故事时，才知道这对他来说是多么绝对的恐怖。他还是个小男孩。那是他的爸爸，却被几个全副武装、穿着制服的男人扑倒并按在地上。他被强行拖走了。在普莉希拉赶来接他之前，O被送去寄养了两天。而且再次地，她又和他分开了。就像那种距离感。那是国际边境。那种痛苦简直让人难以承受。

00:32:47.540 --> 00:32:56.220
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. And so then another thing that you did in your reporting is that you went to Allen's trial for attempted murder.
<v Ira Glass>对。所以在报道中，你做的另一件事就是去旁听了艾伦谋杀未遂案的审判。

00:32:56.220 --> 00:33:32.300
<v M. Gessen>So the trial didn't happen for another 10 months, which is pretty normal. It's in federal court in San Francisco. So I went to the trial. And by that point, I think I fully believed that Allen had taken out a hit on Priscilla. I had tried and convicted him in my mind. But I think most other members of my family, including Priscilla, were waiting for something to emerge during the trial that would make it easier to take, something that would make it seem like not such a horrible thing.
<v M. Gessen>所以审判在10个月后才开始，这其实挺正常的。审判在旧金山的联邦法院进行。所以我去旁听了。到那个时候，我想我已经完全相信艾伦确实买凶要杀普莉希拉了。我在心里已经对他进行了审判并判定他有罪。但我想我家族的其他大多数成员，包括普莉希拉在内，都在等待审判期间出现某种转机，好让事情更容易接受一点，某种能让这件事看起来没那么可怕的东西。

00:33:32.300 --> 00:33:38.620
<v Ira Glass>Like maybe it wasn't true, or maybe it was true in some way that it wasn't quite so bad?
<v Ira Glass>比如也许这不是真的，或者虽然是真的，但某种程度上没那么糟糕？

00:33:38.620 --> 00:33:49.500
<v M. Gessen>Which I can't imagine what it would be. And I'm not sure they could either, but they were sort of holding out hope that something would explain it away.
<v M. Gessen>我简直无法想象那会是什么。而且我不确定他们能不能想象，但他们多少还是抱着一丝希望，希望有什么东西能把这一切合理化。

00:33:49.500 --> 00:33:54.020
